 cluster at Safe/Solid or Likely D across ten raters", "Prediction markets price Sykes at 94-95% to win", "Coleman drew negative coverage over a missed financial disclosure"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a comfortable Democratic margin in a district that leans Democratic by two points, amplified by incumbency and a large fundraising gap. Expert raters and prediction markets agree Sykes is a heavy favorite, so the forecast stays near the fundamentals-based estimate with only a slight trim for the absence of local polling.", "watch": "A late influx of outside spending against Sykes or a strong Republican national environment could narrow the margin enough to move the race toward Likely D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Emilia Sykes (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Emilia Sykes seeks reelection in a northeastern Ohio district that leans Democratic by about two points. She enters the general election with a decisive fundraising advantage, roughly $1.9 million on hand compared with about $78,000 for Republican nominee Carey Coleman, an Army veteran who won the May primary. Coleman has drawn unfavorable coverage for missing a financial disclosure deadline and for remarks about teachers' unions, and he has challenged Sykes to debates. An independent candidate, Sandeep Dixit, is also on the ballot but has raised little money.\n\nNo general-election polling is available, so the outlook rests on fundamentals, expert ratings, and markets. Ratings from ten outlets range from Likely D to Solid or Safe D, and prediction markets put Sykes's chances in the mid-90s. The race is rated Safe D, with the main uncertainty being the national political environment rather than anything specific to this district.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9765, "margin": 13.33}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9641, "margin": 12.5}, {"date": "2026-09